# Making a tree smaller, and getting it back.
#
# A codec is an [encode] and a [decode] over trees, held as `△ encode decode`,
# with `decode (encode t) = t` for every tree. [Codec.Grammar], [Codec.Huffman]
# and [Codec.Term] are the three here, and each exports its pair as `codec`.
# They find different redundancy, or write it at a different cost, and none
# wins everywhere, so which to reach for is the caller's choice
# ([Codec.shrink_best] tries them all); their own tests carry the sizes.
#
# There are two strengths of result, and the difference is not stylistic.
#
# [encode] and [decode] give back the tree itself, so they serve anything: data,
# or a program that is read rather than run. [shrink] gives back a tree that
# only *acts* as the original, applied — it is the original η-expanded, equal to
# it at every argument but not the same tree. So [shrink] is for a function, and
# a tree that will be inspected rather than applied needs the pair.
#
# That is also why [defer] exists rather than `decode c (encode c t)`: the whole
# point is to carry the small tree and not the large one, so the decoding has to
# wait for an argument. Evaluation here is eager, which works out whatever part
# of a λ's body does not use the λ's variable as soon as the λ is — so the
# application that decodes is put under a λ that does use it.

create = Pair.create
encode = Pair.fst
decode = Pair.snd

# [size t]: the count [size] gives, from the children's counts rather than by
# adding along a walk. The evaluator remembers every application, so a subtree
# is counted once however often it occurs: the cost is the tree's DAG, where
# [size]'s is the tree, and the trees worth shrinking are the ones whose DAG is
# far smaller than they are.
size = fix $ \self triage 1 (\c Nat.succ (self c)) (\l \r Nat.succ (Nat.add (self l) (self r)))

# Whichever of the two is the smaller tree, a's being kept on a tie.
smaller = \a \b Bool.match a b (Nat.is_lt (size b) (size a))

# [defer d p]: a tree that acts as whatever the decode [d] makes of p, decoding
# it only once applied — given u it works out `d p` and applies that to u. It
# takes the decode rather than the codec so that a known one is built into the
# tree it returns, instead of a projection out of a pair that the tree would
# then carry and run.
defer = \d \p \u (\q d q u) p
# [shrink c t]: a tree that acts as t, never larger — t itself when the
# encoding does not pay for itself, which for a small tree it will not, the
# decoder being most of what it costs.
shrink = \c \t smaller t (defer (decode c) (encode c t))

# [sizes c t]: what the codec c makes of t, as the sizes of t, of its code,
# and of what [shrink] keeps; [] if the code does not decode back to t. A
# codec's tests list it for trees of every size, one test a tree so that the
# largest is encoded once.
sizes = \c \t let (encode c t) $ \code Bool.match [] [size t; size code; size (shrink c t)] (equal (decode c code) t)

# The identity codec keeps the laws honest without an encoding in the way: what
# a codec and a deferral have to do is the same whether or not anything shrinks.
_id = create id id

_to_string = Bool.to_source
equal (decode _id (encode _id Bool.not)) Bool.not
# = true
# Deferred, it is the function again at its argument, both ways round.
equal (defer id Bool.not true) (Bool.not true)
# = true
equal (defer id Bool.not false) (Bool.not false)
# = true
# An encoding that does not shrink is not taken: shrink hands back the original.
equal (shrink _id Bool.not) Bool.not
# = true
# smaller reads sizes, not values, and keeps the first on a tie.
equal (smaller Bool.not fix) Bool.not
# = true
equal (smaller fix Bool.not) Bool.not
# = true
equal (smaller Bool.not Bool.not) Bool.not
# = true
